From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 18:45:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dovi_ccm: the composer tells the GPU the bounds of the
 integers it multiplies

The composer of ETSI GS CCM 001 (libplacebo-9930) works in integers. A GPU
makes a 32-bit product of two integers it knows nothing of as a full
32-bit multiply, which on AMD's RDNA2 (v_mul_lo_u32) runs at a quarter of
the rate of the other operations; where it knows both factors to be below
2^24 it makes it in one 24-bit multiply at the full rate (v_mul_u32_u24).
Two places of the composer multiply integers whose bounds the shader
knows but did not tell:

- The horizontal pass of annex B.3 (ccm_el_horizontal(), in the luma
  pass and in the chroma pass) filters the codes of the vertical pass,
  which writes float(clamp(v, 0, 65535)): whole numbers below 2^16 that
  its format holds exactly. They are now read with & 0xFFFF, which leaves
  each as it is: the eight products of an odd sample by the constants of
  the filter are made in 24 bits.

- The MMR terms of 5.4.2.3.3 (ccm_chroma()) are products of the three
  samples u, each clamped into [pivots[0], pivots[last]] of its
  component, which pl_dovi_ccm_prepare() checks to be codes of the BL in
  order (0 to 2^BL_bit_depth - 1). u is now taken with
  & (2^BL_bit_depth - 1), which leaves it as it is: the products of the
  terms and those of dv_mul20 are made in 24 bits. The products by the
  coefficients stay 32-bit multiplies: they are of the low 32 bits of
  numbers of up to 32 bits.

The same integers, so the same composed planes and the same pictures.

Measured: the composed planes of the 14 pictures of a profile 7 FEL
stream and every picture drawn from them, by every transport and
configuration of the bridge's checks, the same bytes as before.
radeonsi (ACO, gfx1030 through Mesa's drm-shim), the passes of a
profile 7 FEL picture with an order-3 MMR: the luma pass, 8 32-bit
multiplies to none, ACO's estimate of the cycles of a wave of 64
fragments 118 to 86; the chroma pass, 84 to 42, 1183 to 1001.
---
diff --git a/src/renderer.c b/src/renderer.c
index e864b38..3b0caf5 100644
--- a/src/renderer.c
+++ b/src/renderer.c
@@ -2184,6 +2184,13 @@ static pl_fmt ccm_half(pl_gpu gpu, int n)
 // of the resampled EL codes at the sample p (both chroma components, or the
 // luma in .x). `last`: the last column of the EL picture. `linear`: from the two
 // nearest columns (PL_DOVI_CCM_EL_LUMA_COLUMNS, PL_DOVI_CCM_EL_CHROMA_COLUMNS).
+// (Hades: each code is read with & 0xFFFF, which leaves it as it is: the
+// vertical pass, ccm_el_vertical(), writes float(clamp(v, 0, 65535)), whole
+// numbers that its format holds exactly. A GPU then knows both factors of each
+// product of the filter to be below 2^24 and makes it in one 24-bit multiply
+// at the full rate (RDNA2: v_mul_u32_u24), where a product of a code it knows
+// nothing of is a 32-bit multiply at a quarter of it (v_mul_lo_u32): eight in
+// an odd sample. The same integers.)
 static ident_t ccm_el_horizontal(pl_shader sh, struct ccm_binds *b, pl_tex vert, int last,
                                  bool linear)
 {
@@ -2191,7 +2198,7 @@ static ident_t ccm_el_horizontal(pl_shader sh, struct ccm_binds *b, pl_tex vert,
     ident_t fn = sh_fresh(sh, "dv_el_h");
     GLSLH("ivec2 "$"(ivec2 p) {                                              \n"
           "    int n = p.x >> 1;                                            \n"
-          "#define DV_ELV(k) ivec2(texelFetch("$", ivec2(clamp(n + (k), 0, %d), p.y), 0).xy) \n"
+          "#define DV_ELV(k) (ivec2(texelFetch("$", ivec2(clamp(n + (k), 0, %d), p.y), 0).xy) & 0xFFFF) \n"
           "    ivec2 e;                                                     \n"
           "    if ((p.x & 1) == 0) {                                        \n"
           "        e = DV_ELV(0);                                           \n"
@@ -2485,8 +2492,18 @@ static pl_tex ccm_chroma(struct pass_state *pass, const struct ccm_ctx *x,
             .var = pl_var_ivec3("dv_hi"),
             .data = &(int[3]) { pp->range[0][1], pp->range[1][1], pp->range[2][1] },
         });
-        GLSL("uvec3 u = uvec3(clamp(ivec3(s0, sc), "$", "$"));                  \n",
-             lo, hi);
+        // (Hades: and with & (2^BL_bit_depth - 1), which leaves u as it is: the
+        // clamp takes each sample into [pivots[0], pivots[last]] of its
+        // component, which pl_dovi_ccm_prepare() checks to be codes of the BL,
+        // 0 to 2^BL_bit_depth - 1, in order. A GPU then knows the terms to be
+        // below 2^21 and makes their products, and those of dv_mul20, in
+        // 24-bit multiplies at the full rate (RDNA2: v_mul_u32_u24), where it
+        // made 32-bit ones at a quarter of it (v_mul_lo_u32): 26 of the 84 of
+        // an order-3 chroma sample. The products by the coefficients stay 32-bit
+        // multiplies: those are of the low 32 bits of numbers of up to 32 bits.
+        // The same integers.)
+        GLSL("uvec3 u = uvec3(clamp(ivec3(s0, sc), "$", "$")) & %uu;            \n",
+             lo, hi, (1u << pp->bl_depth) - 1u);
         if (mmrt) {
             // Hades: with the table of the terms of one sample
             // (pl_render_params.dovi_ccm_mmr_table), the terms of two or three
